CloudBurrow patch 74 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1597). Applies after patch 0067; needs 0036,
0041, 0044 and 0065.

The cost: patch 0036 wrote every row a table was given twice, once to the
table and once, with all its values, to its change table, whatever the
table's options. BigQuery's CHANGES needs enable_change_history, but
APPENDS reads any regular table's inserts within its time travel window,
"even if that data is later deleted", and time travel reads any table's
past (https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/time-series-functions,
read 2026-10-17; https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/time-travel), so
every table keeps a history; what it need not keep is a second copy of
the rows it still has unchanged.

The record of a row inserted, or of the new row of an UPDATE, is now
pending: the change table keeps its type, time and row identity, and
googlesqlite_change_pending 1, but no values. The trigger of the change
that next touches the row (UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, TRUNCATE TABLE, a
WRITE_TRUNCATE) writes the row's old values into its pending record
first, so a pending record's values are always its row's in the table.
A statement that names a change table (CloudBurrow's front's APPENDS,
CHANGES, time travel, undelete and rebuild reads) settles its pending
records from the table before it runs, and CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE before
it drops the table it replaces; so every reader reads the history as
before. A change table made before the patch keeps every record's values.

Measured (the engine alone, a table of five columns, 50,000 rows inserted
by 1,000, 10,000 updated, 10,000 deleted): the same insert time (5.9 s,
the analysis dominates), an UPDATE 162 ms (136 before) and a DELETE 104 ms
(74), the first read of the change table 218 ms (100), and the database
39.7 MB (51.9 before; 16.3 with no history at all), a third less for the
history. change_history_pending_test.go fails without the patch and
passes with it.

